  "name": "Public goods game with and without punishment",
  "author": "Human Not Found",
  "description": "Original study: Fehr, E., & Gächter, S. (2000). Cooperation and punishment in public goods experiments. American Economic Review, 90(4), 980-994. Groups of four received 20 tokens per period and chose how much to contribute to a group project returning 0.4 tokens per token to every member, for 10 periods without and 10 periods with a punishment opportunity: after seeing the others' contributions, members could assign costly punishment points that reduced the punished member's earnings. Public goods games appear in 65 of the 500 studies in our survey. This template runs the two treatments as two conditions (between runs, not within the same group). Punishment is linear here: each point costs the punisher 1 token and removes 3 from the punished member (the original used an increasing cost schedule and a percentage reduction), with up to 10 points per member per period. Participants are simulated: each is a Model participant, using the free test model until you choose a model, so the first runs only check the design; with the test model every participant takes the sample answer.",
